-- title:
--   At level one the full and two-generator modular function fields agree
-- statement:
--   Work inside the field of Laurent series $\mathbb{Q}((q))$, written `LaurentSeries ℚ`, and let $\mathrm{jq}$ denote the distinguished element `jq` of it. For a natural number $d \neq 0$, `qExpand ℚ d` is the ring endomorphism of `LaurentSeries ℚ` obtained by re-indexing exponents along multiplication by $d$ on $\mathbb{Z}$ (the substitution $q \mapsto q^{d}$, which is injective and order-preserving on exponents). Two intermediate fields of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$ are attached to a level $N \neq 0$: `modularFunctionField N`, the subfield generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the two elements $\mathrm{jq}$ and `qExpand ℚ N jq`, and `modularFunctionFieldFull N`, the subfield generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the whole set `divisorExpansions N` of elements `qExpand ℚ d jq` for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$. The theorem asserts the equality of these two intermediate fields at level $N = 1$: $$\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldFull}\ 1 = \mathrm{modularFunctionField}\ 1,$$ that is, the field generated by all $\mathrm{jq}(q^{d})$ with $d \mid 1$ coincides with the field generated by $\mathrm{jq}$ together with `qExpand ℚ 1 jq`. There are no further hypotheses.
--
--   This is the level-one case of the comparison between the two presentations of the function field of $X_0(N)$ used in this development: the field generated by all $j(q^{d})$ for $d \mid N$ versus the field generated by $j(q)$ and $j(q^{N})$ alone. It is used in the construction of specialisations of places at level one, via [`ModularCurve.placeSpecialization_exists_level_one_of_surjective`](thm.html#ModularCurve.placeSpecialization_exists_level_one_of_surjective).
